+      kicker: HCV, site: 'sin', title: 'Hepatitis C cirrhosis with CSPH, before treatment',
+      vignette: 'A 52-year-old man with hepatitis C cirrhosis, found when he gave blood, starts sofosbuvir and velpatasvir. He feels well and has never had ascites or bleeding.',
+      line: 'A stiffness of {lsm} with platelets of {plt} puts him in the gray zone, and an HVPG of {hvpg} confirms CSPH, so carvedilol starts with the antivirals.',
+      caveat: OFF_BB,
+      notes: 'Curing hepatitis C does not lower the portal pressure at once, so a patient with CSPH starts carvedilol with the antivirals. In the gray zone between 15 and 25 kPa, Baveno VIII calls CSPH present when a validated model such as ANTICIPATE puts its probability at 75% or more. Here the HVPG settles it.',
+      ask: ['Why start carvedilol now, if the virus will soon be cured?', 'The portal pressure falls only slowly after cure, and carvedilol lowers the risk of decompensation in the meantime.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'year1', days: 365, ramp: { cirrhosis: [0.6, 0.45] }, lapse: { seconds: 8, from: 'Cure', to: 'One year later' }, cam: 'liver',
+      tool: { kind: 'flowtime', clock: 'days', lines: ['hvpg', 'varix'] }, data: 'tiles', tiles: ['hvpg', 'varix', 'lsm', 'plt'], key: ['lsm'], delta: 'before',
+      kicker: HCV, site: 'sin', title: 'A year after cure, the pressure and the stiffness fall',
+      line: 'A year after cure the stiffness reads {lsm}, the platelets have risen to {plt} and the HVPG has fallen to {hvpg}.',
+      notes: 'After cure, inflammation settles first, so the stiffness falls faster than the scar regresses, and an early fall overstates how much fibrosis has gone. The HVPG falls more slowly and less completely, and in many patients with CSPH before treatment it stays at 10 mmHg or more. Whether carvedilol can stop once CSPH resolves is not settled.',
+      ask: ['The stiffness falls fast in the first year after cure. Has the fibrosis regressed as fast?', 'No: much of the early fall is inflammation settling, so the stiffness overstates the regression.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'year2', days: 365, ramp: { cirrhosis: [0.45, 0.37] }, lapse: { seconds: 8, from: 'One year', to: 'Two years after cure' }, cam: 'liver', tool: SCAN,
+      data: 'tiles', tiles: ['lsm', 'plt', 'hvpg'], key: ['lsm'], delta: 'before',
+      kicker: HCV, site: 'sin', title: 'Two years after cure, CSPH is ruled out',
+      line: 'Two years after cure, a stiffness of {lsm}, under {<12 kPa}, with platelets of {plt}, over {>150,000/µL}, rules CSPH out.',
+      notes: 'After hepatitis C is cured, and with no other cause of liver injury such as alcohol or obesity, a stiffness under 12 kPa with platelets over 150,000/µL rules CSPH out, and portal hypertension follow-up can stop. Liver cancer surveillance every 6 months continues, because cirrhosis was present before the cure. Recheck the stiffness and the platelets if a new cause of liver injury appears.',
+      ask: ['Does he still need liver cancer surveillance?', 'Yes, every 6 months: cirrhosis was present before the cure, and the cancer risk remains.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'high', preset: 'csph', params: { cirrhosis: 0.75 }, days: 365, cam: 'liver', data: 'ladder', key: ['hvpg'], tiles: ['varix', 'asc'],
+      kicker: HIGH, site: 'sin', title: 'A higher HVPG before cure',
+      vignette: 'A 58-year-old woman with hepatitis C cirrhosis from a transfusion in 1987 starts sofosbuvir and velpatasvir. EGD shows large varices, and ultrasound shows a little ascites she has not noticed.',
+      line: 'With an HVPG of {hvpg}, large varices and fluid seen only on ultrasound, she starts carvedilol with the antivirals.',
+      caveat: OFF_BB,
+      notes: 'Ascites seen only on ultrasound does not count as decompensation, so she is still compensated, with CSPH and high-risk varices. Carvedilol treats both: it lowers the risk of a first bleed and of decompensation. The higher the HVPG before cure, the more likely CSPH is to persist after it.',
+      ask: ['Ascites seen only on ultrasound: is she decompensated?', 'No: only clinically evident ascites marks decompensation, though even a little fluid on ultrasound carries a worse outlook.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'high2', days: 730, ramp: { cirrhosis: [0.75, 0.57] }, lapse: { seconds: 10, from: 'Cure', to: 'Two years later' }, cam: 'liver', data: 'ladder', key: ['hvpg'], tiles: ['lsm', 'varix'], delta: 'high',
+      kicker: HIGH, site: 'sin', title: 'Two years after cure, CSPH persists',
+      line: 'Two years after cure the HVPG is still {hvpg}, at or above {>=10 mmHg}, so CSPH persists and she keeps carvedilol.',
+      notes: 'When the HVPG starts well above 10 mmHg, CSPH often persists after cure, and so does the risk of decompensation. She keeps carvedilol and liver cancer surveillance every 6 months. Her stiffness, still in the gray zone, cannot tell on its own whether CSPH has gone.',
+      ask: ['Should she stop carvedilol now that the virus is gone?', 'No: CSPH persists, so carvedilol continues.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'masld', preset: 'cirr-comp', params: { cirrhosis: 0.45 }, days: 365, cam: 'liver', tool: SCAN, data: 'tiles', tiles: ['lsm', 'plt'], key: ['lsm'],
+      kicker: MASLD, site: 'sin', title: 'MASLD cirrhosis with obesity sits in the gray zone',
+      vignette: 'A 54-year-old woman with MASH cirrhosis and a BMI of 34 has a liver stiffness of 15 kPa on FibroScan. Her platelets are 136,000/µL, and she has never had ascites or bleeding.',
+      line: 'A stiffness of {lsm} with platelets of {plt} leaves her in the gray zone, where a model that adds her BMI estimates the chance of CSPH.',
+      notes: 'In obese patients with MASH, a stiffness of 25 kPa or more often overstates the portal pressure, so at a BMI of 30 or more it no longer rules CSPH in. Baveno VIII then uses ANTICIPATE-NASH, which adds the BMI to the stiffness and the platelets, and calls CSPH present at a probability of 75% or more. In the model her HVPG is below 10 mmHg.',
+      ask: ['Why does the 25 kPa rule-in not apply to her?', 'At a BMI of 30 or more a high stiffness often overstates the portal pressure, so a model that adds the BMI (ANTICIPATE-NASH) is used instead.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'loss', days: 365, ramp: { cirrhosis: [0.45, 0.35] }, lapse: { seconds: 8, from: 'Weight loss starts', to: 'One year later' }, cam: 'liver', tool: SCAN,
+      data: 'tiles', tiles: ['lsm', 'plt'], key: ['lsm'], delta: 'masld',
+      kicker: MASLD, site: 'sin', title: 'Losing 12% of her weight lowers the stiffness',
+      line: 'A year and 12% of her weight later, the stiffness has fallen to {lsm} and the platelets have risen to {plt}, which now rules CSPH out.',
+      caveat: 'The model has no fat or weight; it eases the scar back instead.',
+      notes: 'In MASLD-related cACLD, losing 10% or more of body weight goes with a fall in stiffness of 30% or more. Diet and exercise come first; a GLP-1 receptor agonist helps where it is indicated, and bariatric surgery is kept for selected patients without CSPH. A stiffness of 15 kPa or less with platelets of 150,000/µL or more rules CSPH out, whatever the cause.',
+      ask: ['How much weight loss changes the stiffness meaningfully?', 'About 10% of body weight or more, which goes with a fall in stiffness of about 30% or more.'],
+    },
+    {
+      id: 'summary', visual: 'table', cols: ['hvpg', 'lsm', 'plt'], asc: false, rowHead: 'Patient',
+      of: [{ id: 'before', kicker: 'Hepatitis C', title: 'Before cure' }, { id: 'year1', kicker: 'Hepatitis C', title: 'One year after cure', vs: 'before' }, { id: 'year2', kicker: 'Hepatitis C', title: 'Two years after cure', vs: 'before' },
+        { id: 'high', kicker: 'Higher start', title: 'Before cure', ref: true }, { id: 'high2', kicker: 'Higher start', title: 'Two years after cure', vs: 'high' },
+        { id: 'masld', kicker: 'MASLD', title: 'Before weight loss', ref: true }, { id: 'loss', kicker: 'MASLD', title: 'After 12% weight loss', vs: 'masld' }],
+      kicker: 'Summary', title: 'How far the pressure falls depends on where it started',
+      line: 'Treating the cause lowers the HVPG in every patient, but CSPH persists when it starts high.',
+      notes: 'Curing hepatitis C, stopping alcohol or losing weight is the most effective long-term treatment of portal hypertension, but the pressure falls over years, not weeks. Patients who start with a high HVPG often keep CSPH and keep their beta-blocker. Liver cancer surveillance continues after any cure in a patient who had cirrhosis.',
+      ask: ['After cure, who still needs carvedilol?', 'Anyone in whom CSPH persists, as shown by the HVPG or by stiffness and platelets.'],
+    },
+  ],
+};
